(Vox OS Core Utilities)
(Initramfs Setup)

A text called "Program Version" is "0.1.0".

(Config)
A number called 'configured wait period on error' is 5 (This is how many seconds we will wait before rebooting).
A text called 'configured welcome message' is "== Welcome to Vox OS ==".
A text called 'configured next action after initramfs has finished' is "/bin/sh".

(Reusable substring search: is " devname\n" present anywhere in hay?
 Vox has no built-in contains/substring operator, only whole-string
 equality (`is`), so this is a hand-rolled sliding-window byte compare.
 The needle is padded with a leading space and trailing newline so a
 match can only occur on a whole /proc/partitions device-name token -
 without both boundaries, "sda1" would also incorrectly match inside
 "sda11", and the bare disk name "nvme0n1" would match every one of
 its own partitions.)
To "contains token" of a buffer called "hay" and a text called "devname".
  a buffer called "needle" is 64 bytes in size.
  copy " {devname}\n" to needle.
  a number called "hay index" is 1. (To track the position of the needle within the hay.)
  a number called "needle index" is 0. (To track the current position within the needle when matching byte-by-byte.)
  a number called "mismatch" is 0. (To track if a mismatch has been found.)
  a number called "hay byte" is 0. (To store the current byte from the hay buffer.)
  a number called "needle byte" is 0. (To store the current byte from the needle buffer.)
  a number called "top" is {hay's size subtract needle's size} add 1. (To determine the maximum starting position for the search.)
  a boolean called "found" is false. (We Found IT!)
  While 'hay index' is less than or equal to top,
      set mismatch to 0 (reset the mismatch),
      set 'needle index' to 1,
      While 'needle index' is less than or equal to needle's size,
          set 'hay byte' to byte {'hay index' add 'needle index' subtract 1} of hay,
          set 'needle byte' to byte 'needle index' of needle,
          if 'hay byte' is not 'needle byte' then, set mismatch to 1.
          increment 'needle index'.
      if mismatch is 0 then, set found to true.
      increment the 'hay index'.
  Return a boolean, found.

(Check a fixed candidate list against a fresh /proc/partitions read.
 Root lives on the SECOND partition of a USB flash drive, not the
 internal NVMe disk. USB mass-storage devices enumerate through the
 SCSI/usb-storage stack as /dev/sdX, never /dev/nvme* - that naming is
 for the internally-attached drive only. USB probe order relative to
 other buses is not guaranteed, so the letter is not necessarily "a" -
 if the internal NVMe controller (or anything else) claims a SCSI-ish
 name first, or the USB drive itself just enumerates later, the flash
 drive could land on sdb, sdc, etc. Checking a small letter range
 covers this without needing to know the exact order in advance.
 Extend this list if your hardware needs a naming scheme not covered
 here - it only needs the bare partition name, not the /dev/ prefix,
 since that is what /proc/partitions itself contains.)
To "find root device" of a buffer called "hay".
  If "contains token" of hay and "sda2" is true then,
      Return a text, "/dev/sda2".
  If "contains token" of hay and "sdb2" is true then,
      Return a text, "/dev/sdb2".
  If "contains token" of hay and "sdc2" is true then,
      Return a text, "/dev/sdc2".
  If "contains token" of hay and "sdd2" is true then,
      Return a text, "/dev/sdd2".
  Return a text, "".

(Setup script for early userspace initialization)
Print "Initramfs setup starting...".

(Create essential directories)
Create a directory called "/sys".
Create a directory called "/dev".
Create a directory called "/run".
Create a directory called "/tmp".
Create a directory called "/newroot".

(Mount virtual filesystems)

Mount "sysfs" at "/sys" with type "sysfs".
On error print "Failed to mount /sys", wait the 'configured wait period on error' seconds, reboot.

Mount "devtmpfs" at "/dev" with type "devtmpfs".
On error print "Failed to mount /dev", wait the 'configured wait period on error' seconds, reboot.

Mount "tmpfs" at "/run" with type "tmpfs".
On error print "Failed to mount /run", wait the 'configured wait period on error' seconds, reboot.

Mount "tmpfs" at "/tmp" with type "tmpfs".
On error print "Failed to mount /tmp", wait the 'configured wait period on error' seconds, reboot.

(Create device nodes if needed)
Create a device node called "/dev/null" with type "c" major 1 minor 3.
Create a device node called "/dev/zero" with type "c" major 1 minor 5.
Create a device node called "/dev/random" with type "c" major 1 minor 8.
Create a device node called "/dev/urandom" with type "c" major 1 minor 9.

(Set up symlinks for compatibility)
Create symbolic link from "/proc/self/fd" to "/dev/fd".
On error print "Failed to create /proc/self/fd symlink".
Create symbolic link from "/proc/self/fd/0" to "/dev/stdin".
On error print "Failed to create /proc/self/fd/0 symlink".
Create symbolic link from "/proc/self/fd/1" to "/dev/stdout".
On error print "Failed to create /proc/self/fd/1 symlink".
Create symbolic link from "/proc/self/fd/2" to "/dev/stderr".
On error print "Failed to create /proc/self/fd/2 symlink".

(Mount /proc so /proc/partitions - ground truth for what the kernel
 has actually enumerated - is readable. A device that appears here
 exists and is mountable by that name under /dev; a device missing
 from here was never detected by any driver, and no amount of waiting
 will ever make it appear.)
Create a directory called "/proc".
Mount "proc" at "/proc" with type "proc".
On error print "could not mount /proc".

(Scan /proc/partitions for a known root device, re-reading it fresh
 each attempt in case a slow-to-enumerate controller needs a moment.
 Falls back to the same interactive "type reboot to give up" escape
 hatch the original hardcoded-device version used.)
a buffer called "partbuf" is 4096 bytes in size.
a text called "root_device" is "".
While root_device is "",
    open a file for reading called "pp" at "/proc/partitions",
    read from pp into partbuf,
    close pp,
    the root_device is "find root device" on partbuf,
    if root_device is "" then,
        Wait 100 milliseconds.

(Mount the real root filesystem)
Mount the root_device at "/newroot" with type "ext4".
On error print "Failed to mount root filesystem", wait the 'configured wait period on error' seconds, reboot.

(pivot_root requires put_old to exist INSIDE the new root - it lives on
 the freshly mounted filesystem, so it can only be created now)
Create a directory called "/newroot/oldroot".

(Move virtual filesystems to new root)
Mount "/dev" at "/newroot/dev" with type "none" with options "move".
Mount "/run" at "/newroot/run" with type "none" with options "move".
Mount "/proc" at "/newroot/proc" with type "none" with options "move".
Mount "/sys" at "/newroot/sys" with type "none" with options "move".

(Switch to new root using pivot_root)
Pivot root to "/newroot" with old root "/newroot/oldroot".
On error print "Failed to pivot_root",  wait the 'configured wait period on error' seconds, reboot.

(Change to new root directory)
Change directory to "/".
On error print "Failed to chdir to new root", wait the 'configured wait period on error' seconds, reboot.

(Release the old initramfs root. Lazy detach: the mount is still busy
 while this program runs from it, so MNT_DETACH defers the release
 until the last user - this process - is gone.)
Unmount "/oldroot" lazily.
On error print "Failed to detach old root", wait the 'configured wait period on error' seconds, reboot.

(init success hand-over)
Print the 'configured welcome message'.
Execute the 'configured next action after initramfs has finished'.
On error print "Failed to execute init", wait the 'configured wait period on error' seconds, reboot.

(Should never reach here)
Print "Initramfs setup complete. Exiting.".
Exit 1.
